def merge_two_sorted(l1, l2):
    dummy = tail = Node(0)
    while l1 and l2:
        if l1.val <= l2.val:
            tail.next, l1 = l1, l1.next
        else:
            tail.next, l2 = l2, l2.next
        tail = tail.next
    tail.next = l1 or l2  # attach whichever list has leftovers
    return dummy.next
```

O(m + n) time, O(1) extra space — no new nodes are allocated, existing nodes are re-linked. The dummy head removes the special case of deciding the very first node of the merged list, same trick as elsewhere in this group. The `tail.next = l1 or l2` line matters: once one list is exhausted, the other's remaining nodes are already sorted and can be attached wholesale.

