def dedup_sorted(head):
    curr = head
    while curr and curr.next:
        if curr.val == curr.next.val:
            curr.next = curr.next.next  # skip the duplicate
        else:
            curr = curr.next
    return head
```

Because the list is already sorted, every duplicate of a value sits immediately adjacent to it — so a single forward pass comparing each node to its immediate neighbor is enough; no hash set of seen values is needed, unlike the unsorted case. O(n) time, O(1) space. Note `curr` only advances when no duplicate was found — advancing unconditionally would skip checking the newly-linked next node against `curr` again.
